{"step_index":183,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:54+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:54+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prover_eval/t0_baseline.py`\nTotal Lines: 135\nTotal Bytes: 5543\nShowing lines 1 to 60\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/prover_eval/t0_baseline.py — deterministic Lean automation, no model.\n3: \n4: T0 answers a question that has to be settled *before* any GPU is spent: how\n5: much of the benchmark does existing Lean automation already solve? Every item\n6: T0 resolves is an item on which all seven provers will score alike, so it\n7: carries no information about them. If T0 clears the false half outright, then\n8: `RefutationSuccess` is a ceiling rather than a measurement and the benchmark's\n9: discriminating power lives entirely in the true half and in direction choice.\n10: \n11: For a refuted conjecture this is not a proof search at all. The counterexample\n12: graph is explicit and finite, so the refutation is a kernel computation::\n13: \n14:     intro h; have := @h (Fin 8) _ _ Gcex _; revert this; decide\n15: \n16: which either reduces or does not. That is exactly what makes the false half\n17: cheap — and exactly why it may fail to separate models.\n18: \n19: Usage:\n20:     python tools/prover_eval/t0_baseline.py [--partition gold] [--limit N]\n21: \"\"\"\n22: from __future__ import annotations\n23: \n24: import argparse\n25: import json\n26: import os\n27: import sys\n28: import time\n29: \n30: sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(\n31:     os.path.abspath(__file__)))))\n32: \n33: from config import CONFIG  # noqa: E402\n34: \n35: \n36: def battery_columns():\n37:     import pandas as pd\n38:     return list(pd.read_parquet(\n39:         os.path.join(CONFIG.cache_dir, \"seed_battery.parquet\")).columns)\n40: \n41: \n42: def main() -> int:\n43:     ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n44:     ap.add_argument(\"--benchmark\", default=\"benchmark\")\n45:     ap.add_argument(\"--partition\", default=\"gold\")\n46:     ap.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=None)\n47:     ap.add_argument(\"--timeout\", type=int, default=900)\n48:     ap.add_argument(\"--out\", default=\"\")\n49:     args = ap.parse_args()\n50:     CONFIG.lean_timeout_s = args.timeout\n51: \n52:     from pipeline import lean_disproof\n53:     from pipeline.theorem_prover import LeanSubprocessProver\n54: \n55:     items = json.load(open(os.path.join(args.benchmark,\n56:                                         f\"{args.partition}.json\")))\n57:     if args.limit:\n58:         items = items[:args.limit]\n59:     columns = battery_columns()\n60: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":184,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:54Z","thinking":"**Evaluating Benchmark Discriminability**\n\nThe analysis points out that if the model easily refutes the false conjectures, the RefutationSuccess metric becomes a ceiling. This means the benchmark's ability to discriminate relies entirely on the true conjectures and the choice of direction.\n\nFor a refuted conjecture, the refutation is a direct kernel computation, rather than a full proof search. The counterexample graph is finite, making the refutation process explicit.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean","EndLine":537,"StartLine":495,"toolAction":"View radius definitions","toolSummary":"View radius in GraphInvariantsComputable.lean"}}]}
{"step_index":185,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:57Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:57+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:57+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 495 to 537\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n495:   (((univ.filter (IsDoubleRomanDominatingFunction G)).image\n496:     doubleRomanWeight).min).getD 0\n497: \n498: /- ── radius ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n499: \n500: /-- Vertices within `k` steps of `v`, by iterated neighbourhood expansion.\n501: Distance is not available to the kernel directly (`SimpleGraph.dist` is defined\n502: through `sInf` over walk lengths), so it is reconstructed as reachability. -/\n503: def ballWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ → V → Finset V\n504:   | 0,       v => {v}\n505:   | (k + 1), v =>\n506:       let B := ballWithin G k v\n507:       B ∪ B.biUnion (fun u => univ.filter (fun w => G.Adj u w))\n508: \n509: /-- Eccentricity of `v`: least `k` with every vertex within `k` steps. -/\n510: def eccentricity (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) : ℕ :=\n511:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k => ballWithin G k v = univ)\n512: \n513: def radius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n514:   ((univ.image (eccentricity G)).min).getD 0\n515: \n516: /-- Undefined on a disconnected graph, where some vertex never reaches all of\n517: `univ` and `minUpTo` would fall back to `n`. networkx raises there. -/\n518: def HasRadius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n519:   ∀ v : V, ballWithin G (Fintype.card V) v = univ\n520: \n521: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n522:     Decidable (HasRadius G) := by unfold HasRadius; infer_instance\n523: \n524: /- ── graph construction from an explicit edge list ────────────────────────── -/\n525: \n526: /-- The graph on `Fin n` with the given edge list, symmetrised and loop-free.\n527: This is the shape the disproof exporter emits for a refuting witness.\n528: \n529: `SimpleGraph.fromRel` supplies the symmetry and irreflexivity proofs, so this\n530: stays a definition rather than a bundle of obligations that would have to be\n531: re-discharged (and re-adjusted across mathlib versions) in generated code. It is\n532: an `abbrev` so that `DecidableRel` synthesis can see through it at the use site. -/\n533: abbrev ofEdges (n : ℕ) (es : List (Fin n × Fin n)) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) :=\n534:   SimpleGraph.fromRel (fun u v => (u, v) ∈ es)\n535: \n536: end GraphCalc\n537: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
